#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124357 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124548 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f00311f-e61b-5845-b19b-59cfbdb5871a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/id #uuid "1a085da3-d8eb-52eb-94ea-4969fb74a83f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5876" :question/problem "What is the domain of $f(x) = \\sqrt{3x+5}-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y = \\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $g(x) = \\sqrt{3x+5} -2,$ we replace $x$ with $3x+5$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n{3x+5} \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+5 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge -5 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{5}{3}, \\infty\\right).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6f94991ce01ad98ec5bdffbb0673182dc40bc264803b6eea5154241985359c0b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124358 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124549 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc785eb1-cb7e-5368-a092-51f87b7f7c35" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bf9e3a92-47fa-5969-8d05-2d28114f3036" :question/math-academy-id "e-5877" :question/problem "Which of the following values are possible outputs of $f(x) = 2 \\sqrt{x- 2} + 2?$\n\nI. $1$\n\nII. $2$\n\nIII. $6$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\sqrt x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt x \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts do not affect the range. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x-2} \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$ and then adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x-2}+2 & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in [2,\\infty).$\n\nAmong the given values, only $f(x)=2$ and $f(x)=6$ lie in the range. So, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10d9a611e2fbf8ebf6ae3c12b57e51ea100bf13663586379c42293d4ee45d183.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124359 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124550 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5e23379-0f5d-5691-b10f-5125325ffa70" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformed Square Root Function Given Some Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "cdb0a166-a880-5efb-a747-ac8f940ff50b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5878" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-x}+c.$ Now consider the following properties of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- The domain of $f(x)$ is $x \\in (-\\infty, 2].$\n\n- The range  is $f(x) \\in [2, \\infty).$\n\n- The point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-x}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b-x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\le b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in \\left(-\\infty, 2\\right],$ which is equivalent to $x\\le 2.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\nb=2.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{a\\sqrt{2-x}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=2.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =a\\sqrt{2-1}+2 \\\\ 4 & =a+2 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=2\\sqrt{2-x}+2,$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c = 2 + 2 + 2 = 6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124389 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124581 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58b93ad7-d548-58cb-9cc5-0bcd79e28e4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/id #uuid "fa7bd088-d496-5862-80a0-cd20083b2754" :question/math-academy-id "e-7944" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x) = \\sqrt{6x+7} - 5.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6x+7} - 5 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6x+7}-5 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{6x+7} & =5 \\\\ (\\sqrt{6x+7})^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ 6x+7 & =25 \\\\ 6x & =18 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6\\cdot 3+7}-5 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{25} & =5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124548 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f00311f-e61b-5845-b19b-59cfbdb5871a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cf67c77-91be-5eab-8ec1-78a1c10468d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556497} {:answer/id #uuid "2989486c-c7fa-5cdc-bb39-cb0786fa5299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556518} {:answer/id #uuid "a555140a-4b9d-55d9-add9-863f5e16350e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556831} {:answer/id #uuid "ca80941b-902e-523a-b089-d3daa893d689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556915} {:answer/id #uuid "e9575243-29dc-5258-82da-6a73e79a5aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cf67c77-91be-5eab-8ec1-78a1c10468d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556497} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec4a6373-b04b-55f6-92d5-cafe236a4c92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e99c86a-1348-545b-9c9f-3a1428e5141e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115372" :question/problem "What is the domain of $f(x)=5\\sqrt{4x-3}?$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)=5\\sqrt{4x-3},$ we replace $x$ with $4x-3$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n4x-3\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge 3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in [\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f71e9e717ab5c217d91528de89540de9013eadd31b1907c89d43d589fb6929af.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124548 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f00311f-e61b-5845-b19b-59cfbdb5871a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7de831af-f1a9-5f3e-99cf-e02ebbbe0a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556719} {:answer/id #uuid "a5687a27-5a30-5f0c-9a00-6c27a6816f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ is not defined for any value of $x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556832} {:answer/id #uuid "abd2a011-9345-5dac-9bfe-0db3bac72332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556853} {:answer/id #uuid "daa71957-017a-5538-a1a5-204f82a67c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556973} {:answer/id #uuid "ec31db76-c5fd-5689-a0f5-26cde5e7f348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daa71957-017a-5538-a1a5-204f82a67c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556973} :answer-field/id #uuid "94127993-da95-546e-8e26-790d927d8bcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acd7fcaf-37e0-5893-ba70-06ebe65a32f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-115377" :question/problem "What is the domain of $f(x)=3\\sqrt{8x}-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)=3\\sqrt{8x}-2,$ we replace $x$ with $8x$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n8x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ef/efbc925cd1aca7f476d6cfc11f3c668c4f3bde78acf359eb0a65696ce2a12852.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124549 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc785eb1-cb7e-5368-a092-51f87b7f7c35" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dfb396d-19bb-50d9-9137-780c44346eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{9}{5}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556641} {:answer/id #uuid "7904ba0f-9797-5697-8431-88692532adb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556709} {:answer/id #uuid "b14d19e8-60b0-529d-84f5-d4d38745c586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [\\frac{9}{5},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556864} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c19164-5b86-5641-a5ff-8f0edcd2b282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in (-\\infty ,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556877} {:answer/id #uuid "d54b5fee-97ea-5670-9b88-ecf5c742bd92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d54b5fee-97ea-5670-9b88-ecf5c742bd92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\in [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556947} :answer-field/id #uuid "844a1e28-73b3-57bb-9963-fbd57ccb007b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4cbeb52a-0392-5f34-918d-f2e44920e406" :question/math-academy-id "q-115385" :question/problem "What is the range of $f(x)=3\\sqrt{5x+9}?$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5x+9}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5x+9} & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{5x+9} & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8ece70a3c8855e00a0232248cb922a40c8c9756ef67ea12b012eb719c8ee3bd1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124581 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58b93ad7-d548-58cb-9cc5-0bcd79e28e4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0165f473-4337-566d-9694-678006847e88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556433} {:answer/id #uuid "340fd56f-9ed8-59f8-b237-f9fd54e9e9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no roots" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556540} {:answer/id #uuid "a1244121-62dc-5583-9fd2-3bd580b02f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556823} {:answer/id #uuid "b075542e-07c0-57e6-bb78-c2cf0fac2d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{31}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556863} {:answer/id #uuid "edf4dfe7-2aae-50c7-b758-39a68bace86c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "340fd56f-9ed8-59f8-b237-f9fd54e9e9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no roots" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556540} :answer-field/id #uuid "b07284bb-0da9-5e96-a0b2-03339339ef3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "821416d3-f1f2-5d6c-b79b-722f962867ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-115388" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=2\\sqrt{5x+10}+3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{5x+10}+3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5x+10}+3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5x+10} & =-3 \\\\ \\sqrt{5x+10} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no roots."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124550 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5e23379-0f5d-5691-b10f-5125325ffa70" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformed Square Root Function Given Some Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081ed565-5d0f-501c-9498-afa689f056b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556447} {:answer/id #uuid "1b39913b-30d3-5924-9a1a-74b2bcaffb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556491} {:answer/id #uuid "3246c862-4dad-537f-bda3-2329a7720373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556535} {:answer/id #uuid "a80e8513-ebe8-5049-adf1-1a72124229f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556846} {:answer/id #uuid "c761f43f-536d-5834-b501-01b7c6317e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c761f43f-536d-5834-b501-01b7c6317e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556910} :answer-field/id #uuid "1df867aa-158a-5470-96c7-2a305d7a3650" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31d9f406-b6d6-5a07-bd60-36de4e4c1d5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115542" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-3x}.$ Now consider the following properties of $f(x):$\n\n- The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,1].$\n\n- The point $(-11,-24)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\nFind the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-3x}\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b-3x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -3x & \\ge -b \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{b}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,1],$ which is equivalent to $x\\le 1.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{3}=1\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\n- Since the point $(-11,-24)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-11) & =a\\sqrt{3-3\\cdot (-11)} \\\\ -24 & =6a \\\\ a & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=-4\\sqrt{3-3x}$ and\n\n$$\na+b=-4+3=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124549 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc785eb1-cb7e-5368-a092-51f87b7f7c35" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe7df7b8-dd76-588d-bf96-d1a367df5b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe7df7b8-dd76-588d-bf96-d1a367df5b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557058} :answer-field/id #uuid "69cb606f-66c9-5c21-b497-a4aa6183e686" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557127} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfd9fad1-7e48-533a-9de4-6d8341265adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfd9fad1-7e48-533a-9de4-6d8341265adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556931} :answer-field/id #uuid "db6d0703-93cf-564b-a9f8-5d2287a0d22d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24750a35-9998-5176-94af-1ab0c0c1ac7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-274961" :question/problem "Determine the range of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{x-3}+3.$\n\nThe range is $f(x)$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts do not affect the range. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x-3}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $\\frac{3}{5}$ and then adding $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x-3} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{x-3} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{x-3}+3 & \\ge 3 \\\\ f(x) & \\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in [3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c27b96134ab2d7004262d14163db2cd3714fd0c18fb6bb84444252d5bdac2ef7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124548 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f00311f-e61b-5845-b19b-59cfbdb5871a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34ea3ac2-bb33-5310-9978-d13df8149805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34ea3ac2-bb33-5310-9978-d13df8149805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556544} :answer-field/id #uuid "0938b589-eeeb-5c28-b350-d0d6ca254011" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557068} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17c6ccb6-e4f8-51ea-8ba5-2b34ab064602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17c6ccb6-e4f8-51ea-8ba5-2b34ab064602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556481} :answer-field/id #uuid "af617755-880c-5549-a29f-1cfbdcfef121" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ddedbc01-5d9e-5e7a-be28-fa7766a765e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-277275" :question/problem "Determine the domain of $f(x)=\\sqrt{6x-5}.$\n\nThe domain is $x\\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)=\\sqrt{6x-5},$ we replace $x$ with $6x-5$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n6x-5\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-5 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 6x & \\ge 5 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in [\\frac{5}{6},\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/4d/4d0a6205a61aa0625a082d6d3bc1941ab00e0536c85844f28af873798bf6db64.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124548 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f00311f-e61b-5845-b19b-59cfbdb5871a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e30c897-3ba0-5fe2-b2fb-4154efb87814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e30c897-3ba0-5fe2-b2fb-4154efb87814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556642} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a429198-aa6a-503b-8694-c555670b935b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ceeba4da-f0c2-5be1-9c89-f0c0346bd63b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceeba4da-f0c2-5be1-9c89-f0c0346bd63b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556927} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f52f454-d984-5e42-98ea-448aa919b6ed" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "363ec8e4-c2ba-5e32-b1d7-37d1b114d1fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-277276" :question/problem "Determine the domain of $f(x)=4\\sqrt{7+x}+1.$\n\nThe domain is $x\\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)=4\\sqrt{7+x}+1,$ we replace $x$ with $7+x$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n7+x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in [-7,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b5b24146a009936bb2fab85afa4f40b888881e4d6978fb76e76e7180b2cd83d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124581 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58b93ad7-d548-58cb-9cc5-0bcd79e28e4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122464dc-3073-5ef0-91f2-6ff298d4d6e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "122464dc-3073-5ef0-91f2-6ff298d4d6e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556467} :answer-field/id #uuid "90fcae54-4761-5bbd-a196-a62c3cc597fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be201399-7461-56d1-865b-23d766571416" :question/math-academy-id "q-277380" :question/problem "The root of the function $f(x)=-\\sqrt{8-2x}+4$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{8-2x}+4=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{8-2x}+4 & =0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{8-2x} & =-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{8-2x} & =4 \\\\ (\\sqrt{8-2x})^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ 8-2x & =16 \\\\ -2x & =8 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{8-2(-4)}+4 & =0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{8+8}+4 & =0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{16}+4 & =0 \\\\ -4+4 & =0 \\\\ -4+4 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124550 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5e23379-0f5d-5691-b10f-5125325ffa70" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformed Square Root Function Given Some Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "813db01e-cf21-5292-addd-723299c9c19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2x-8}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "813db01e-cf21-5292-addd-723299c9c19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2x-8}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556728} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5ff4797-7c2a-514d-aa3d-bf7bbb62b553" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0bb9e173-3e0e-5cad-b727-d2df13c7ef82" :question/math-academy-id "q-277438" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=a\\sqrt{2x+b}+c.$ Now consider the following properties of$f(x):$\n\n- The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in [4,\\infty ).$\n\n- The range is $f(x)\\in [-1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The point $(6,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\nTherefore, our function is\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{2x+b}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+b & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge -b \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in [4,\\infty ),$ which is equivalent to $x\\ge 4.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\n-\\frac{b}{2}=4\\,⟹\\,b=-8.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{a\\sqrt{2x-8}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=-1.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(6,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =3 \\\\ a\\sqrt{2(6)-8}-1 & =3 \\\\ 2a-1 & =3 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=2\\sqrt{2x-8}-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124581 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58b93ad7-d548-58cb-9cc5-0bcd79e28e4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e88954f-957b-57f5-9bbc-ba8c17ee7bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no roots" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556569} {:answer/id #uuid "4466e510-4cb0-5e44-a6d4-09673d1ccf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556581} {:answer/id #uuid "54903bff-703a-5f3d-a66b-a7747bb332e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556624} {:answer/id #uuid "97378096-9630-55ac-b7bc-b4e0bef588b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556793} {:answer/id #uuid "9962bede-e69b-568e-b720-68ae9609dfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e88954f-957b-57f5-9bbc-ba8c17ee7bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no roots" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556569} :answer-field/id #uuid "df9ae1bc-cf9c-58da-99a8-99c8f9f9fe90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b5454cb-d7d1-50b2-8aa5-cfa1e1b3686a" :question/math-academy-id "q-89991" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt{3x+1}+4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3x+1}+4=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3x+1}+4 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x+1} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no roots."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124581 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58b93ad7-d548-58cb-9cc5-0bcd79e28e4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82d1ab47-c4b7-5eac-a183-d992bc2821aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556733} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6fc057-f8d1-5fda-ade0-1932cd94d9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556854} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2ebb7e-eb95-5bb7-84aa-bc1a83877f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no roots" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556918} {:answer/id #uuid "ef923bb4-26d8-57af-bda9-9f8afcaf5ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557020} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9e175e-3cbb-5dda-8735-b54de7fb4cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb2ebb7e-eb95-5bb7-84aa-bc1a83877f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no roots" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556918} :answer-field/id #uuid "74bb54bf-b891-5fc6-b056-efc04e739628" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6dd5656-22b2-5e5b-b391-f41ec70a470b" :question/math-academy-id "q-89992" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt{4-5x}+10.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4-5x}+10=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-5x}+10 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4-5x} & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no roots."}]}
